+# Specification Quality Checklist: Log Redaction CLI
+
+**Purpose**: Validate specification completeness and quality before proceeding to planning
+**Created**: 2026-11-05
+**Feature**: [spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+**Review Ownership**: This checklist is a reviewer-owned requirements-quality review artifact. Mark an item `[x]` only when the reviewer determines the requirements-quality criterion is satisfied.
+**Marker Semantics**: `[x]` means the criterion has been reviewed and satisfied for requirements quality. It does not mean implementation work is complete.
+
+## Content Quality
+
+- [x] No implementation details (languages, frameworks, APIs) — see Note 1
+- [x] Focused on user value and business needs
+- [x] Written for non-technical stakeholders
+- [x] All mandatory sections completed
+
+## Requirement Completeness
+
+- [x] No [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] markers remain — resolved in iteration 2
+- [x] Requirements are testable and unambiguous
+- [x] Success criteria are measurable
+- [x] Success criteria are technology-agnostic (no implementation details)
+- [x] All acceptance scenarios are defined
+- [x] Edge cases are identified
+- [x] Scope is clearly bounded
+- [x] Dependencies and assumptions identified
+
+## Feature Readiness
+
+- [x] All functional requirements have clear acceptance criteria
+- [x] User scenarios cover primary flows
+- [x] Feature meets measurable outcomes defined in Success Criteria
+- [x] No implementation details leak into specification
+
+## Constitution Alignment (v1.0.0)
+
+- [x] Principle I (Standard Library Only): runtime dependency constraint recorded (A-10); no
+      third-party runtime dependency implied by any requirement
+- [x] Principle II (Fail Loudly): error behaviour stated for every invalid-input case —
+      unreadable file (FR-012), unrecognised option (SR-001), unreadable standard input (A-6),
+      failed output write (SR-009)
+- [x] Principle III (Every Requirement Is Testable): every FR/SR stated in observable terms;
+      SC-002 requires a complete requirement-to-test mapping
+- [x] Principle IV (Simplicity): A-9 forecloses configuration, extension points, and options
+      beyond `--count`; A-8 bounds the redaction categories
+- [x] Principle V (Secure by Default): dedicated Security Requirements section (SR-001..SR-009)
+      mapped to the Security Baseline controls, each stated testably
+- [x] Security Baseline — resource limits: enforced maximum input size fixed at 1 MiB per line
+      with memory-bounded detection (SR-005) and a fail-loud refusal path (FR-018)
+
+## Validation Iterations
+
+**Iteration 1 (2026-11-05)**: Initial spec reviewed. Findings and resolutions:
+
+1. *Overlap behaviour between the three redaction kinds was unspecified* — two different
+   implementations could produce different output for the same line. Resolved by adding FR-014
+   (fixed email → IPv4 → card precedence, redact once, never re-scan placeholders) and
+   Assumption A-2 recording why.
+2. *Fragment matching was unspecified* — whether a Luhn-valid 16-digit substring inside a longer
+   digit run is a card, and whether a dotted quad inside a version string is an IP. Resolved by
+   adding FR-013 and the corresponding edge cases.
+3. *Card separator rules were ambiguous* — "groups separated by single spaces or single hyphens"
+   did not say whether separators could be mixed within one number. Resolved in FR-006 (one
+   separator character throughout) with the rejected shapes listed under Edge Cases.
+4. *Byte-level output fidelity was implied but not required* — resolved by FR-008, FR-009, and
+   the edge cases covering trailing newlines, CRLF, and invalid UTF-8 (A-4).
+5. *Security requirements were absent*, which Principle V makes a blocking defect. Resolved by
+   adding SR-001..SR-009 and this Constitution Alignment section.
+6. *Resource limits* — the Security Baseline mandates an enforced maximum size per input, and no
+   reasonable default reconciles that with FR-009's promise that every line is emitted.
+   **Not resolved in this iteration**: raised as Question 1 for the requester.
+
+**Iteration 2 (2026-11-05)**: Question 1 answered — requester delegated the decision with the
+single constraint that it be handled safely. Resolution:
+
+1. SR-005 now fixes an enforced maximum single-line length of 1 MiB inclusive, and requires that
+   detection of an over-length line be itself memory-bounded (no unbounded read before refusal).
+2. FR-018 added: an over-length line stops the run with a diagnostic naming the input source and
+   exit status 2; no part of the line reaches standard output, and no `--count` summary is
+   written, so a partial tally cannot be mistaken for a complete one.
+3. FR-009 amended to name FR-018 as its one exception, so the two requirements no longer conflict.
+4. Edge case, User Story 4 scenarios 6-8, SC-010, and Assumption A-12 added to cover the cap
+   boundary (exactly 1 MiB accepted), the refusal path, the suppressed summary, and the rationale
+   for refusing over chunking.
+
+All 18 checklist items and all 6 constitution-alignment items now pass. No [NEEDS CLARIFICATION]
+markers remain.
+
+## Notes
+
+- **Note 1 (implementation details)**: The only technology names in the spec are the requester's
+  explicit delivery constraints — the invocation `python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]` in
+  FR-001, which is the tool's user-facing contract, and the runtime/packaging constraint recorded
