+# Feature Specification: Log Redaction CLI
+
+**Feature Branch**: `001-logredact-cli`
+
+**Created**: 2026-11-05
+
+**Status**: Draft
+
+**Input**: User description: "Build a command-line tool that removes personal data from log files before they are shared. Run it as `python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]`. It reads each FILE in the order given, or standard input when no FILE is given, and writes every line to standard output with emails replaced by `[EMAIL]`, IPv4 addresses replaced by `[IP]`, and Luhn-valid 13-19 digit payment card numbers (optionally grouped with single spaces or hyphens) replaced by `[CARD]`. Everything else is output unchanged. With `--count`, a summary `emails=N ips=N cards=N` is written to standard error after all output. If a FILE cannot be read, an error naming the file goes to standard error and the exit status is 2; otherwise the exit status is 0. Python 3.11+, standard library only, package `logredact` under `src/`."
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*
+
+### User Story 1 - Redact a piped log stream (Priority: P1)
+
+An engineer is about to paste a chunk of application log into a ticket. They pipe the log through
+the tool and get back the same text with every email address, IPv4 address, and payment card
+number replaced by a fixed placeholder, so the text is safe to share while still being readable
+and diffable against the original.
+
+**Why this priority**: This is the whole purpose of the tool and the smallest useful slice. With
+only this story implemented the tool already prevents personal data from leaving the
+organisation. Every other story is a convenience layered on top.
+
+**Independent Test**: Feed a known line on standard input with no arguments and compare standard
+output byte-for-byte against the expected redacted line. Delivers the core protective value with
+no file handling and no flags.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** no FILE arguments, **When** `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7\n` is written to
+   standard input, **Then** standard output is exactly `user [EMAIL] from [IP]\n` and the exit
+   status is 0.
+2. **Given** no FILE arguments, **When** `paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123\n` is
+   written to standard input, **Then** standard output is exactly
+   `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123\n` (the Luhn-failing 13-digit run is untouched).
+3. **Given** no FILE arguments, **When** a line containing no email, no IPv4 address, and no
+   Luhn-valid card number is written to standard input, **Then** standard output is that line,
+   byte-for-byte unchanged.
+4. **Given** no FILE arguments, **When** `host 999.1.1.1 and 192.168.0.10\n` is written to
+   standard input, **Then** standard output is exactly `host 999.1.1.1 and [IP]\n`.
+5. **Given** no FILE arguments, **When** standard input is empty, **Then** standard output is
+   empty and the exit status is 0.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 2 - Redact named log files in order (Priority: P2)
+
+An operator has several rotated log files to hand to a vendor. They name the files on the command
+line in the order they want them read and capture one redacted stream, rather than redacting each
+file by hand.
+
+**Why this priority**: It is the common real-world invocation, but it is additive — the redaction
+behaviour it depends on is already proven by Story 1.
+
+**Independent Test**: Create two small files with known content, run the tool with both names, and
+confirm standard output is the redaction of the first file followed by the redaction of the
+second. Testable without `--count` and without error cases.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** files `a.log` and `b.log` exist and are readable, **When** the tool is run with
+   `a.log b.log`, **Then** standard output is the redacted contents of `a.log` followed
+   immediately by the redacted contents of `b.log`, and the exit status is 0.
+2. **Given** files `a.log` and `b.log` exist, **When** the tool is run with `b.log a.log`,
+   **Then** the output order is reversed accordingly (operand order is honoured, not sorted).
+3. **Given** one FILE argument is given, **When** the tool runs, **Then** standard input is not
+   read at all.
+4. **Given** the same file name is given twice, **When** the tool runs, **Then** its redacted
+   contents appear twice in standard output.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 3 - See how much was redacted (Priority: P3)
+
+A reviewer needs to confirm that redaction actually happened — and roughly how much — before
+approving a log for release. They add `--count` and read a one-line tally on standard error,
+leaving the redacted log on standard output clean enough to redirect to a file.
+
+**Why this priority**: It is a verification aid, not a protective function. The tool is already
+safe without it.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run with `--count` over input with a known number of each kind of match and
+assert the exact standard-error line, while standard output is unaffected by the flag.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** `--count` is supplied, **When** `a@b.io 1.2.3.4\n` is read from standard input,
+   **Then** standard output is exactly `[EMAIL] [IP]\n` and standard error is exactly
+   `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0\n`.
+2. **Given** `--count` is supplied, **When** the input contains no redactable values, **Then**
+   standard error is exactly `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0\n` and the exit status is 0.
+3. **Given** `--count` is supplied and multiple FILE operands are read, **When** the run finishes,
+   **Then** the summary reports the totals across all operands, not per file.
+4. **Given** `--count` is not supplied, **When** the run succeeds, **Then** nothing is written to
+   standard error.
+5. **Given** `--count` is supplied, **When** standard output is redirected to a file, **Then** the
+   summary does not appear in that file.
+
+---
+
+### User Story 4 - Fail loudly on an unreadable file (Priority: P3)
+
+An operator mistypes a file name or points at a file they cannot read. Instead of silently
+producing a short, apparently-complete redacted log, the tool tells them which file it could not
+read and exits with a failure status their script can detect.
+
+**Why this priority**: Low frequency, but a silent partial redaction would be mistaken for a
+complete one, which is the most expensive failure mode this tool has.
+
+**Independent Test**: Run the tool naming a path that does not exist and assert the exit status is
+2 and that standard error identifies that path.
+
+**Acceptance Scenarios**:
+
+1. **Given** a FILE operand naming a path that does not exist, **When** the tool runs, **Then**
+   standard error contains a message that includes that path as written on the command line, and
+   the exit status is 2.
+2. **Given** a FILE operand naming a path whose permissions deny reading, **When** the tool runs,
+   **Then** standard error contains a message naming that path and the exit status is 2.
+3. **Given** a FILE operand naming a directory, **When** the tool runs, **Then** standard error
+   contains a message naming that path and the exit status is 2.
+4. **Given** an unreadable FILE, **When** the tool exits, **Then** standard error contains no
+   Python traceback and no contents of any input file.
+5. **Given** an unrecognised option such as `--verbose`, **When** the tool runs, **Then** standard
+   error describes the rejected option, nothing is written to standard output, and the exit status
+   is non-zero.
+6. **Given** an input whose third line is 1 MiB + 1 characters long, **When** the tool runs,
+   **Then** the first two lines appear redacted on standard output, no part of the third line
+   appears on standard output, standard error names the input source and reports that a line
+   exceeded the maximum length, and the exit status is 2.
+7. **Given** an input containing a line of exactly 1 MiB including its terminator, **When** the
+   tool runs, **Then** that line is redacted and emitted normally and the exit status is 0.
+8. **Given** an over-length line and the `--count` flag, **When** the tool exits with status 2,
+   **Then** no `emails=N ips=N cards=N` summary is written, so a partial tally cannot be mistaken
+   for a complete one.
+
+---
+
+### Edge Cases
+
+- **Adjacent and overlapping candidates**: a value that could be read as more than one kind of
+  personal data (for example a digit run that is both a Luhn-valid card and contains dots) is
+  redacted exactly once, under the fixed precedence in FR-014. Placeholders already written are
+  never themselves re-scanned or re-counted.
+- **Digits adjacent to a card candidate**: a 22-digit run that happens to contain a Luhn-valid
+  16-digit substring is not a card number — a card candidate must not be flanked by another digit
+  (FR-013).
+- **Separator shape**: `4111 1111 1111 1111` and `4111-1111-1111-1111` are card numbers;
+  `4111  1111  1111  1111` (double spaces), `4111 1111-1111 1111` (mixed), and
+  `4111--1111--1111--1111` are not card candidates under FR-006 and are left alone.
+- **Octet boundaries**: `0.0.0.0` and `255.255.255.255` are IPv4 addresses; `256.1.1.1`,
+  `999.1.1.1`, `1.2.3`, and `1.2.3.4.5` are not and are left alone.
+- **Dotted version-like numbers**: `10.0.0.7` inside a longer token such as `v10.0.0.7-beta` —
+  behaviour is fixed by FR-013 (an IPv4 candidate must not be flanked by a digit or a dot).
+- **Email shapes**: `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` is redacted. A trailing sentence period
+  (`mail jane@example.com.`) leaves the period in place.
+- **Last line without a trailing newline**: the final line is emitted without a newline the tool
+  did not receive; no newline is added and none is removed.
+- **CRLF and other line endings**: the bytes that terminate each line are passed through
+  unchanged.
+- **Bytes that are not valid UTF-8**: such bytes are passed through to standard output unchanged
+  rather than causing a failure or being replaced (Assumption A-4).
+- **Empty input / empty file**: produces empty output, exit status 0, and (with `--count`) an
+  all-zero summary.
+- **Very long single line**: a line of up to 1 MiB is redacted and emitted normally. A longer line
+  is refused rather than processed: the run stops with a diagnostic naming the source and exit
+  status 2 (FR-018), and none of that line reaches standard output. A line exactly at the cap is
+  accepted; the cap is inclusive.
+- **Downstream pipe closed early** (for example `| head -1`): the tool stops without a traceback
+  and without claiming success it did not achieve (Assumption A-7).
+- **A later FILE is unreadable after earlier ones were written**: output already written to
+  standard output stays written; the run still ends with status 2 (FR-012), so a caller must treat
+  a non-zero status as "output is incomplete".
+
+## Requirements *(mandatory)*
+
+### Functional Requirements
+
+- **FR-001**: The tool MUST be invocable as `python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]` and MUST
+  accept zero or more FILE operands.
+- **FR-002**: With no FILE operand, the tool MUST read its input from standard input.
+- **FR-003**: With one or more FILE operands, the tool MUST read every operand in the order given
+  and MUST NOT read standard input.
+- **FR-004**: The tool MUST replace every email address in the input with the literal `[EMAIL]`.
+  An email address is a local part of one or more characters drawn from letters, digits, and
+  `. _ % + -`, followed by `@`, followed by a dot-separated domain of letter/digit/hyphen labels
+  whose last label is at least two letters (so `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` is redacted).
+- **FR-005**: The tool MUST replace every IPv4 address in the input with the literal `[IP]`. An
+  IPv4 address is four dot-separated decimal numbers, each 0-255 with no leading zeros beyond a
+  single `0`. A dotted quad with any component outside 0-255 (for example `999.1.1.1`) MUST be
+  left unchanged.
+- **FR-006**: The tool MUST replace every payment card number in the input with the literal
+  `[CARD]`. A card number is 13 to 19 decimal digits, written either as an unbroken run or as
+  groups of digits joined by a single space or a single hyphen, where the same separator
+  character is used throughout, and whose digits pass the Luhn checksum.
+- **FR-007**: A digit sequence that matches the shape of FR-006 but fails the Luhn checksum MUST
+  be left unchanged (for example `1234567890123`).
+- **FR-008**: Every character of input that is not part of a redacted match MUST be written to
+  standard output unchanged, in its original order and position relative to the placeholders.
+- **FR-009**: A line containing nothing to redact MUST be written to standard output byte-for-byte
+  as it was read, including its line terminator or the absence of one on a final line. The single
+  exception is a line exceeding the length cap of SR-005, which is governed by FR-018.
+- **FR-010**: With `--count`, the tool MUST write, after all redacted output has been written and
+  flushed, exactly one line to standard error of the form `emails=N ips=N cards=N`, where each N
+  is the total number of replacements of that kind made across all inputs in the run.
+- **FR-011**: Without `--count`, a successful run MUST write nothing to standard error.
+- **FR-012**: If any FILE operand cannot be opened or read, the tool MUST write a message to
+  standard error that includes that file name as given on the command line, and MUST exit with
+  status 2.
+- **FR-013**: Redaction MUST NOT match a fragment of a larger token: a card candidate MUST NOT be
+  immediately preceded or followed by a decimal digit, and an IPv4 candidate MUST NOT be
+  immediately preceded or followed by a decimal digit or a dot.
+- **FR-014**: When two candidate matches would overlap, the tool MUST apply a single fixed,
+  documented precedence — email, then IPv4 address, then card number — and MUST redact the
+  overlapping region exactly once, counting it once, under the winning kind. Text already
+  replaced by a placeholder MUST NOT be re-scanned.
+- **FR-015**: Any run that is not an error MUST exit with status 0.
+- **FR-016**: The tool MUST process input as a stream of lines, so that a run over an input far
+  larger than available memory completes without accumulating the whole input.
+- **FR-017**: The tool MUST NOT modify, truncate, or write to any FILE operand.
+- **FR-018**: On encountering a line longer than the SR-005 cap, the tool MUST stop processing,
+  write a message to standard error that names the input source (the file operand as given, or
+  standard input) and states that a line exceeded the maximum length, and exit with status 2. No
+  part of the over-length line — redacted, unredacted, or truncated — may be written to standard
+  output, and no `--count` summary is written for a run that ends this way. Output already
+  written for earlier lines remains written; the non-zero status is what tells the caller the
+  output is incomplete.
+
+### Security Requirements
+
+Derived from OWASP ASVS and the project Security Baseline; each is a functional requirement and
+MUST have at least one automated test.
+
+- **SR-001**: Command-line arguments MUST be validated against an allowlist — the single option
+  `--count`, the `--` end-of-options separator, and file operands. Any other option MUST be
+  rejected with a diagnostic on standard error and a non-zero exit status, with nothing written to
+  standard output (ASVS V5.1 input validation; Principle II fail-loudly).
+- **SR-002**: All input — command-line arguments, file contents, and standard input — MUST be
+  treated as untrusted data. No part of any input may be interpreted as a command, a format
+  string, a path to construct another path from, or code. The tool MUST NOT invoke a shell or any
+  subprocess.
+- **SR-003**: Diagnostics MUST NOT disclose personal data. An error message may name the file
+  operand the caller supplied, but MUST NOT include file contents, any input line, any matched
+  email address, IP address, or card number, or any fragment of one. No Python traceback may reach
+  standard error on a handled error path.
+- **SR-004**: The `--count` summary MUST consist only of the three integer totals in the FR-010
+  format. It MUST NOT include sample values, line numbers, or any other content derived from the
+  redacted data.
+- **SR-005**: Resource consumption MUST be bounded for any single input. Peak memory MUST be
+  bounded by one line at a time rather than by total input size (FR-016), and every pattern used
+  for matching MUST run in time linear in the length of the line, with no input able to trigger
+  catastrophic backtracking. The tool MUST enforce a maximum single-line length of 1 MiB
+  (1,048,576 characters, including the line terminator); a longer line MUST be handled per
+  FR-018. Detection of an over-length line MUST itself be memory-bounded: the tool MUST NOT read
+  more than the cap plus a small fixed margin of a single line into memory before rejecting it.
+- **SR-006**: Redaction MUST be complete, not partial: a redacted value MUST be replaced in full
+  by its placeholder, leaving no digits, characters, or length information from the original value
+  in the output.
+- **SR-007**: The tool MUST NOT write input data anywhere other than standard output and MUST NOT
+  create temporary files, caches, or log files containing input data.
+- **SR-008**: FILE operands MUST be opened read-only and exactly as supplied, with no path
+  construction, no globbing, and no following of a path outside what the caller named.
+- **SR-009**: A failure to write output MUST NOT be silently discarded: the tool MUST NOT report
+  success for a run whose redacted output was not fully written.
+
+### Key Entities
+
+- **Input source**: either a named file operand or standard input; carries the name used in any
+  diagnostic about it, and the order in which it is read.
+- **Line**: the unit of reading and writing; a run of input text with its original terminator,
+  processed independently of every other line.
+- **Redaction kind**: one of email, IPv4 address, or card number; each has a recognition rule, a
+  fixed placeholder (`[EMAIL]`, `[IP]`, `[CARD]`), and a precedence rank.
+- **Match**: a region of a line identified as one redaction kind, which is replaced by that kind's
+  placeholder and increments that kind's counter exactly once.
+- **Run tally**: three non-negative integers, one per redaction kind, accumulated over the whole
+  run and reported only when `--count` is given.
+
+## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*
+
+### Measurable Outcomes
+
+- **SC-001**: All three worked examples in the feature description reproduce byte-for-byte on
+  standard output and standard error, with the stated exit statuses — 3 of 3.
+- **SC-002**: 100% of FR and SR items in this specification map to at least one automated test,
+  with no unmapped requirement at the implementation gate.
+- **SC-003**: Across a corpus of lines containing no personal data, 100% are returned
+  byte-for-byte identical to the input.
+- **SC-004**: Across a corpus of near-miss values (out-of-range dotted quads such as `999.1.1.1`,
+  Luhn-failing digit runs, digit runs shorter than 13 or longer than 19, mis-separated groupings,
+  non-email `@` usages), 0 are redacted.
+- **SC-005**: Across a corpus of genuine emails, IPv4 addresses, and Luhn-valid card numbers in
+  all the groupings FR-006 allows, 100% are redacted and none appears in any form in the output.
+- **SC-006**: A run over an input at least 100 times larger than the tool's peak memory completes
+  successfully, and peak memory does not grow with total input size.
+- **SC-007**: Every trial that names an unreadable file ends with exit status 2 and a standard
+  error message containing that file name — 100% of trials.
+- **SC-008**: In every trial, no email address, IP address, card number, or fragment of one
+  appears on standard error — 100% of trials, including error and `--count` runs.
+- **SC-009**: A reviewer can confirm from the `--count` summary alone how many values of each kind
+  were removed, without opening the redacted output.
+- **SC-010**: Peak memory for any single run stays within a fixed bound of roughly the 1 MiB line
+  cap regardless of input size or line length, and no input — including a single line of 1 GB —
+  can make the tool exhaust memory or emit any part of an over-length line.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- **A-1** (counting): Counts are per replacement made, not per distinct value and not per line; a
+  value appearing three times counts three times.
+- **A-2** (precedence): The email → IPv4 → card precedence of FR-014 is chosen because an email's
+  domain or local part can contain dotted-quad-like and digit-run-like text, so matching emails
+  first avoids carving a redacted email into pieces.
+- **A-3** (ordering of streams): The `--count` summary is written after standard output has been
+  flushed, so a caller watching both streams sees the complete log before the tally, as the
+  feature description requires.
+- **A-4** (encoding): Input is decoded as UTF-8 and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are passed
+  through to standard output unchanged, so a log with a few corrupt bytes is still redacted rather
+  than rejected. Redaction rules operate on ASCII-range characters only.
+- **A-5** (exit status for bad usage): An unrecognised option exits with status 2, matching the
+  only failure status the feature description names, rather than introducing a second one.
+- **A-6** (standard input is never "unreadable"): A read failure on standard input is reported the
+  same way as a file read failure, naming the input as standard input, and exits with status 2.
+- **A-7** (closed downstream pipe): If standard output is closed early by the consumer, the tool
+  stops writing and terminates by the usual convention for that signal, without a traceback; this
+  is not counted as a successful complete run.
+- **A-8** (scope): Only the three categories named are in scope. Names, phone numbers, MAC
+  addresses, IPv6 addresses, national identifiers, and URLs containing credentials are explicitly
+  out of scope for this feature.
+- **A-9** (no configuration): Placeholders, patterns, and the output format are fixed. There is no
+  configuration file, no environment variable, and no option other than `--count`.
+- **A-10** (runtime constraint, given by the requester and required by project Principle I): the
+  tool runs on Python 3.11 or newer using only the standard library, as the package `logredact`
+  under `src/`, runnable via `python -m logredact` with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. This is a stated
+  constraint on the delivery, not a design choice made in this specification.
+- **A-11** (verification): Automated tests are written with `pytest`, the one development
+  dependency the project constitution permits.
+- **A-12** (line cap, decided on the requester's delegation to handle this safely): the cap is
+  1 MiB per line and an over-length line fails the run (FR-018). Reasoning: refusing is the only
+  option that cannot produce a silent redaction gap. Chunking a long line would bound memory too,
+  but a value straddling a chunk boundary could escape redaction — un-redacted personal data in
+  output that looks clean, which is precisely the failure this tool exists to prevent. Omitting
+  the cap would leave peak memory controlled by the input, which the Security Baseline forbids.
+  1 MiB is roughly 500x a typical syslog line and well above a single-line JSON payload, so the
+  refusal path should be unreachable for real logs; when it does fire, it is loud and the caller
+  learns which source was at fault.